\pard\b\fs20 The Creation\b0\par
\fs16\par
In the beginning was Wuotan. He was alone in the void.\par
Then he created two beings, who are called his children. One was a daughter, named Sawel\'f4; the other was a son, to whom he gave the name Teiwaz. Then Ea came into being, the fabric of the world, as a place for his children. Here he built Midgard. Midgard was now under the command of Sawel\'f4 and Teiwaz. They wandered through the empty lands of Midgard, she like a woman of graceful beauty, he like a proud, strong man. Together they filled the empty lands of Midgard. Sawel\'f4 planted trees and shrubs; Teiwaz folded the land into mountains and filled hollows with water. She watered the plants with the water of the clouds, and he dug caves into the mountains.\par
Now the land flourished in the splendor of its lakes and forests, mountains and valleys. From the union of Sawel\'f4 and Teiwaz after this successful work, she now gave birth to twins, Ask and Embla, the first creatures to walk on Midgard. Then Sawel\'f4 created the animals from the earth: birds, hares and game, to feast on the plants, to the delight of the twins.\par
But Teiwaz liked to tease his sister, and so he created wild beasts of prey that fell upon Sawel\'f4's creatures. She was hurt, and with earthquakes she made her brother's mountains collapse. The answer came as burning streams of lava across the land, scorching bush and tree.\par
When Wuotan learned of his children's quarrel, he was deeply saddened. He separated the squabblers and banished them from Midgard. Sawel\'f4 was banished to Asgard, west of Midgard, and Teiwaz to Utgard, in the east of Midgard. But Ask and Embla stayed on Midgard and were given dominion over the land. They populated it and were from then on called humans.\par
Sawel\'f4 and Teiwaz, however, were denied being with their children, the grandchildren of Wuotan. Each of them could only influence their fate from afar. Teiwaz cursed them and gave them old age, so that it would carry them off. But Sawel\'f4 cared for her children and let them multiply, so that they would renew themselves again and again and defy old age.\par
